          zfswatcher - ZFS pool monitoring and notification daemon

                           version history


version 0.9.0 (unreleased)

New features

 - Webhook notifications: new sections [webhook "name"] with "enable",
   "level", "url" (http or https), "preset", "token" and "throttle". The
   messages of one check run are sent as one HTTP POST request. Presets:
   generic (JSON, default), ntfy, gotify, discord and slack. The token is
   sent as bearer token (ntfy, generic) or X-Gotify-Key (gotify). A request
   times out after 10 s and is retried once after a network error or HTTP
   5xx; failures are logged with log/slog (without token and query string).
   Attachments are not sent. Examples are in the configuration files.
 - "zfswatcher --test-notify" sends a test message (with the most urgent
   configured level, so it reaches every channel) to all enabled outputs and exits without starting
   the daemon. The exit code is 1 if a webhook delivery fails.
 - Prometheus endpoint "<rootdir>/metrics" (text format), enabled with the
   new option "metrics = true" in [www] (default false). Role viewer; HTTP
   Basic or an API token works (tokens are now accepted for the paths
   below "<rootdir>/api/" and for "<rootdir>/metrics"). Metrics: pool and
   device states, device error counters, pool space, time and result of
   the last check. See the section "Prometheus" in README.md.


version 0.8.0 released 2026-11-22

Upgrade notes

 - The unfinished iostat stream was removed. The option "zpooliostatcmd" in
   section [main] is obsolete: it is still accepted so that existing
   configuration files keep working, but it has no effect and zfswatcher
   logs a warning at startup and on reload if it is set. Remove it from
   your configuration.
 - Web users now have a role (new option "role" in [wwwuser]): "viewer",
   "operator" or "admin". Without "role" a user is an admin, so existing
   configurations behave as before. A viewer sees the dashboard, status,
   usage, statistics, enclosure and about pages, but not the logs and has
   no locate buttons; an operator may additionally see the logs and
   switch locate LEDs. An invalid role is a configuration error.
 - The web interface now uses Bootstrap 5. The CSS class maps in [www]
   (severitycssclassmap, poolstatecssclassmap, devstatecssclassmap,
   usedstatecssclassmap) use the new class names in the example
   configurations: text-error -> text-danger, text-muted ->
   text-body-secondary, progress-bar-info/-warning/-danger/-success (and
   bar-* of the illumos example) -> bg-info/bg-warning/bg-danger/bg-success,
   and for log lines error/danger -> table-danger, warning -> table-warning,
   info -> table-info, success -> table-success. Existing configuration
   files with the old names keep working: zfswatcher translates them when
   the configuration is loaded, without a warning. Custom templates in an
   own templatedir must be ported to Bootstrap 5 and must not use inline
   scripts or style attributes any more (the Content-Security-Policy no
   longer allows inline styles).
 - New option "refresh" in [www] (default 10): the dashboard and the pool
   status reload their content every N seconds (2 to 3600, 0 = off; other
   values are configuration errors). The page works without it.

New features

 - JSON API (read only, GET): "<rootdir>/api/v1/info", "pools",
   "pools/{name}", "usage" (viewer) and "events" (operator, optional
   "?limit=N"). Errors are JSON {"error": "..."}. Basic login and API
   tokens work. The API may still change until version 1.0; see the
   section "JSON API" in README.md.
 - API tokens: new sections [apitoken "name"] with "enable", "hash"
   ("sha256:" and 64 hex digits) and "role". A token is sent as
   "Authorization: Bearer <token>" and is accepted only for paths below
   "<rootdir>/api/". Password login
   works for the API as well.
 - "zfswatcher -T" (--generate-token) creates a new API token, prints it
   once and shows the matching "hash = sha256:..." line for the
   configuration.

Changes

 - Web interface: Bootstrap 3.4.1 and jQuery 3.7.1 were replaced by
   Bootstrap 5.3.9 (new layout, responsive, works on phones) and htmx 2.0.11
   for the automatic refresh of the dashboard and the pool status (no
   jQuery any more). The pages have no inline scripts or styles, so the
   Content-Security-Policy no longer contains 'unsafe-inline' for styles.
   The enclosure page is rendered on the server from layout data, without
   JavaScript; its slots are filled by the device name ("1-3" is slot 1-3),
   no longer by the position in the list. The background image was removed.
 - Internal diagnostics (debug output such as runtime statistics on
   SIGUSR1, and internal errors of the notifier library) are now written
   with log/slog to stderr, which is the journal under systemd
   ("journalctl -u zfswatcher"). The debug level is enabled with -d.
   Messages a human has to act on still go to the configured log files,
   syslog and mail.
 - A successful reload (SIGHUP, also sent by logrotate after rotating the
   log files) is logged as "configuration reloaded" at level info. Before,
   only a debug message "reconfiguring, reopening logs" was written.

Fixes

 - zfswatcher no longer starts a background "zpool iostat" process that
   only printed its output to stdout.

Packaging and CI

 - Bootstrap 5.3.9 and htmx 2.0.11 are bundled; jQuery and Bootstrap 3
   were removed from the source tree (debian/copyright is updated).
 - The .deb is built with xz compression. The CI runner defaulted to zstd,
   which lintian rejects.
 - The CI minimum test coverage was raised to 68 %.

Internal changes

 - The notifier package has a plug-in interface for output channels
   (notifier.Output and Notifier.AddOutput, documented in
   notifier/doc.go). Log file, syslog, e-mail, standard output and
   callback outputs are now implemented with it. The configuration and
   the behaviour are unchanged.
 - New data layer (model.go): exported types Pool (with the devices as a
   tree), Device, Usage, Event and HostInfo without any presentation
   details, and snapshot functions which return copies of the current
   state. The web pages are built from these snapshots; the pages are
   unchanged. The log buffer of the web interface now stores time and
   severity instead of preformatted strings. This is the basis for a
   JSON API.


version 0.7.0 released 2026-11-22

Upgrade notes

 - zfswatcher now saves the pool state to "statefile" (default
   /var/lib/zfswatcher/state.json; "statefile =" disables it) and after a
   restart reports only the changes since the last run. The first start
   after the update has no state file yet and therefore reports every pool
   and device once more. The bundled systemd unit creates the directory
   (StateDirectory=); with a custom unit or init script the directory must
   be writable for the service user. The file is written atomically with
   mode 0640.
 - The web interface has a new health check at "<rootdir>/healthz", which
   needs no login (see below). Restrict access to it in the reverse proxy
   if you do not want it to be public.
 - Failed web logins are now logged as warnings with the client address.
 - A reverse proxy must pass the original Host header on ("proxy_set_header
   Host $http_host;" in nginx), otherwise the CSRF protection rejects
   state changing requests. The new option "trustedproxies" is only needed
   if the log should show the real client address.

New features

 - State persistence: zfswatcher remembers the pool state across restarts
   (see upgrade notes) and reports only the changes since the last run.
 - Health check at "<rootdir>/healthz" for monitoring tools such as Uptime
   Kuma. It needs no login and reveals nothing about pools: 200 "ok" if the
   last "zpool status" query worked and is not older than three times
   "zpoolstatusrefresh", otherwise 503 with "starting", "stale" or "error".
 - New option "commandtimeout" in section [main]: the time in seconds
   (1 to 3600, default 60) after which a hanging zpool or zfs command is
   killed. It is read at startup only.
 - New option "trustedproxies" in section [www]: IP addresses or networks
   of reverse proxies whose X-Forwarded-For and X-Forwarded-Proto headers
   are trusted. The default (empty) ignores these headers. The README has
   nginx and Caddy examples.

Fixes

 - The web server shuts down gracefully on SIGTERM and SIGINT: running
   requests get up to 5 seconds to finish and no new ones are accepted.
 - A pool whose configuration cannot be determined is now shown without
   devices instead of with a bogus device named "The".
 - The iostat stream reader no longer loses entries that arrive together
   in a single read.
 - A failed or malformed iostat read no longer crashes the daemon.

Packaging and CI

 - Removed etc/gccgo-build.sh, which built from the bundled golibs/ tree.
 - The project homepage and the RPM spec now point to the maintained fork
   at github.com/tantefred/zfswatcher, and the about page names the fork.
 - The RPM spec documents the Go 1.26 requirement but does not check it as
   a build dependency, so the package builds with a Go toolchain from
   elsewhere.
 - More parser tests, including real zpool output of degraded pools and
   data corruption (scenario F); CI enforces a minimum test coverage.


version 0.6.0 released 2026-11-22

Upgrade notes

 - Web passwords: MD5 crypt hashes ("$1$...") keep working but are
   deprecated and logged at startup; create a bcrypt hash with
   "zfswatcher -P" and put it into your configuration. If a web user still
   has the example password "toor", zfswatcher warns at startup and the web
   pages show a red notice.
 - "zpoolstatusrefresh" and "zfslistrefresh" must be between 1 and 86400
   seconds; other values are now rejected as configuration errors.
 - Configurations from 0.4.x with the old 9 column zfslistcmd and
   zfslistusagecmd work again, but updating them to the commands of the
   example configuration is recommended (zfswatcher logs a warning).
 - Switching LEDs on or off ("locate") in the web interface needs POST and
   rejects cross-origin requests; a script calling it with GET must be
   changed. State changing requests are checked against the Origin header,
   so a reverse proxy has to pass the Host header (and Origin) unchanged.
 - New log files are created with mode 0640; existing files keep their mode.

Security

 - Web interface hardening: the HTTP server has timeouts and a header size
   limit, all responses carry security headers (Content-Security-Policy,
   X-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy, and HSTS with
   TLS). Switching an LED on or off ("locate") now requires POST, rejects
   cross-origin requests (CSRF), accepts only known devices and no longer
   redirects to the Referer header.

 - Web passwords are now stored as bcrypt hashes ("$2a$..."). "zfswatcher -P"
   reads the password without echo (twice on a terminal, one line from
   stdin otherwise) and prints a bcrypt hash; the example configuration uses
   a bcrypt hash of "toor". The unmaintained github.com/abbot/go-http-auth
   is no longer used: HTTP Basic authentication is built in, and unknown or
   disabled users take as much time to reject as wrong passwords.

 - zfswatcher logs a warning at startup and the web pages show a red notice
   if an enabled web user still has the example password "toor".

 - Bundled web assets updated: Bootstrap 3.3.7 -> 3.4.1 (fixes several XSS
   issues, CVE-2018-14040, CVE-2018-14042, CVE-2019-8331) and jQuery
   3.2.1 -> 3.7.1. The bundled Bootstrap license file is now the MIT
   license text. The dead html5shim script (loaded over http, for IE 8 and
   older) is removed from the web pages.

 - E-mail notifications use the standard library net/smtp instead of the
   unmaintained github.com/snabb/smtp. STARTTLS is used when the server
   offers it, connections time out after 30 seconds, and the messages now
   carry Message-ID, MIME-Version and UTF-8 headers (non-ASCII subjects are
   encoded). The configuration is unchanged.

 - New log files are created with mode 0640 instead of 0666.

Fixes

 - Configurations from 0.4.x with the 9 column zfslistcmd/zfslistusagecmd
   (without logicalused and compressratio) work again; since 0.5.0 all
   usage data and the used space warnings were missing for them. The
   dashboard shows "-" for the unknown columns and zfswatcher logs a warning
   once, recommending to update the commands to the example configuration.

 - Errors are no longer ignored silently. A pid file which cannot be written
   (for example an old "pidfile = /var/run/zfswatcher.pid" while running as
   the user zfswatcher) is now logged as an error; the daemon keeps running.
   A failing "locate" request in the web interface is logged and answered
   with an error. "zpoolstatusrefresh" and "zfslistrefresh" must be between
   1 and 86400 seconds; other values are rejected as configuration errors.

Packaging and CI

 - New man page zfswatcher-ledctl(8). The Debian postinst no longer runs
   "chown -R" on /var/log/zfswatcher; systemd sets the ownership via
   LogsDirectory= when the service starts.

 - The FreeBSD and Solaris builds are now vetted in CI; FreeBSD without cgo
   compiles with stubs (the real FreeBSD binary still needs cgo).

version 0.5.0 released 2026-11-21

 - First release of the tantefred fork. Linux (Debian, Proxmox VE) first.

 - Builds with Go modules; the bundled golibs/ directory is gone.

 - Understands current OpenZFS (2.x) output: "0B" sizes, remove:, checkpoint:,
   expand: and condense: sections, scrub and resilver reported together,
   special, dedup, log, cache and spare classes, dRAID, and the file list of
   "zpool status -v".

 - Fixed wrong arguments in the "device state changed" notifications.

 - The Debian package runs the daemon as the unprivileged system user
   "zfswatcher" with a systemd unit (the SysV init script is gone). The
   unit creates /var/log/zfswatcher and hardens the service. The bundled
   zfswatcher-ledctl and its udev rule are installed, so enclosure LEDs work
   without root. If you use ledctl from the ledmon package, add User=root
   with "systemctl edit zfswatcher". Logrotate now rotates
   /var/log/zfswatcher/*.log.

 - The web interface now listens on 127.0.0.1:8081 by default instead of
   all interfaces on port 80, for use behind a reverse proxy. Change "bind"
   in the [www] section to listen elsewhere, for example ":8081" for all
   interfaces. Ports below 1024 need CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE for the service
   user (see the comment in the systemd unit).

 - The Linux binary is static and built without cgo. It no longer crashes
   with "Illegal instruction" on virtual CPUs without AVX.

 - External commands (zpool, zfs) are killed after 60 seconds, so a hanging
   "zpool status" no longer stops the monitoring.

 - Pools in state SUSPENDED now raise an alert. If you maintain your own
   /etc/zfs/zfswatcher.conf, add SUSPENDED:alert to "poolstatemap" and
   SUSPENDED:text-error to "poolstatecssclassmap"; states missing from these
   maps are treated as severity info.

 - Test suite with real Proxmox VE and Debian 13 fixtures, CI with lint,
   formatting, race detector and vulnerability checks.


version 0.3 released 2013-02-20

 - Disk space usage warnings at configurable thresholds.

 - Experimental FreeBSD and OpenIndiana support.

 - Reconfigure on SIGHUP.

 - Ubuntu ZFS PPA dependency fix.

 - Other minor improvements.


version 0.2 released 2013-02-04

 - Compatibility fix for changes in ZoL 0.6.0-rc14 output formatting.

 - Other minor improvements.


version 0.01 released 2013-02-03

 - Initial release.

